%T A399432 13,1,11,13,1,11,13,1,11,13,1,11,13,17,1,11,13,17,1,11,13,17,19,1,11,
%U A399432 13,17,19,1,11,13,17,19,1,13,17,19,1,13,17,19,23,1,13,17,19,23
%N A399432 Irregular triangle read by rows: integers from 1 to n that share no prime factor with any other integer from 1 to n.
%C A399432 To construct row n, begin with the integers 1, 2, ..., n. Simultaneously delete every integer that shares a prime factor with at least one other integer in the same row. Read the surviving integers in increasing order.
%C A399432 Every composite number > 1 is removed because it shares a prime factor with one of its proper divisors.
%C A399432 Every prime p <= n/2 is removed because its multiple 2*p also belongs to the row.
%C A399432 Every prime p > n/2 survives because no other multiple of p occurs in the row.
%C A399432 Therefore, row n consists exactly of 1 followed by all primes p satisfying n/2 < p <= n.
%C A399432 Every prime p appears p times. It enters the array at row p, increasing the row sum by p. It stays until row 2*p-1. At row 2*p, it disappears, decreasing the row sum by p. The sum stays unchanged whenever no prime enters or leaves. Thus, reading the changes in order, the increases are (2,3,5,7,11,...), and the decreases are (-2,-3,-5,-7,-11,...). Every prime causes exactly one increase and one later decrease of the same size.
%C A399432 Start with any row n. Repeatedly halve the row number, dropping any fractional part, until reaching row 1. Ignore the entry 1 in each selected row. Together, the remaining entries contain every prime not exceeding n, exactly once.
%e A399432 Table begins:
%e A399432   1;
%e A399432   1, 2;
%e A399432   1, 2, 3;
%e A399432   1, 3;
%e A399432   1, 3, 5;
%e A399432   1, 5;
%e A399432   1, 5, 7;
%e A399432   1, 5, 7;
%e A399432   1, 5, 7;
%e A399432   1, 7;
%e A399432   ...
%o A399432 (PARI) isok(m,n) = my(v=select(x->x!=m, [1..n])); if (#v, for (i=1, #v, if (gcd(m, v[i]) != 1, return(0))); 1); 1;
%o A399432 row(n) = select(m->isok(m,n), [1..n]); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Oct 18 2026
%K A399432 nonn,tabf,new
%O A399432 1,3
%A A399432 _Ali Sada_, Oct 16 2026
